<main id="main" class="wrap web-page"><header><p class="eyebrow"><a href="web.html">Nexo Web</a></p><h1>API and data</h1><p class="lede">Read and update backend data with typed requests and explicit loading and error states.</p><nav aria-label="Web guides"><p><a href="web-guide.html">Components and routing</a> · <a href="web-api.html">API and data</a> · <a href="web-development.html">Development</a> · <a href="web-deployment.html">Deployment</a></p></nav></header><section id="data"><h2>Typed asynchronous data</h2>
<p><code>load</code> owns a reactive GET; <code>mutation</code> owns an explicit POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE. Both use nonblocking browser fetch, exact JSON contracts and visible loading/error states. A successful mutation reloads its explicitly named reads.</p>
<p><a href="web-m3-demo/">Try the read-only data demo</a>. It fetches a real static JSON file. Writes are disabled there; run the complete local project below to test CSRF-protected creation and invalidation.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>data/app.nexo</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code class="nexo">@@DATA@@</code></pre></div>
<p>Data is available only inside a <code>ready</code> or mutation <code>success</code> branch. Errors expose kind, message and HTTP status. <code>reload</code>, <code>cancel</code> and mutation <code>run</code> are explicit operations. Views support conditions and immutable list snapshots; replacing a list resets its child state. Keyed reconciliation remains future work.</p>
<p>GET supports bounded timeouts/retries, consumer-aware deduplication and opt-in memory caching. Requests and cache belong to one mounted application; unmount cancels its work. Changing user/session requires destroying and remounting the root. JSON int values must fit the safe wire integer range; use str for larger identifiers.</p>
<div class="code"><pre><code>python -m nexo web check --json examples/web/data/app.nexo
python -m nexo web build examples/web/data/app.nexo -o dist
python examples/web/data/server.py --directory dist
# Open http://127.0.0.1:8765/
python -m pytest tests/test_web_data.py</code></pre></div>
<p><a href="web-m3-demo/source/app.nexo">Nexo source</a> · <a href="web-m3-demo/source/server.py">Local demo server</a> · <a href="web-m3-demo/source/nexo.toml">Project config</a> · <a href="https://github.com/marcrabadan/nexo/blob/main/docs/rfc/0001-nexo-web.md#m3">Data semantics and limitations</a></p>
<p>Requests allow same-origin paths only, with HTTPS in production. Writes require a server-injected CSRF token and backend Origin/token validation. Credentials use same-origin cookies. The bundled Python server binds loopback and demonstrates session isolation; it has no production authentication, TLS or deployment support. Backend authorization and rate limits remain essential.</p></section><section id="api-reference"><h2>Browser API reference</h2>
<p>Declare requests inside a component or page. The URL is one positional <code>str</code> argument; options are named. These browser APIs are separate from the server-side <code>nexo.web</code> library.</p>
<table><caption>HTTP requests and JSON contracts</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Declaration</th><th scope="col">Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>load name = api.get(url, as: T)</code></th><td>Starts on mount and reacts to scalar URL input changes. No request body.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>mutation name = api.post(url, body: value, as: T)</code></th><td>Starts only when <code>name.run</code> is invoked. PUT and PATCH use the same options.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>mutation name = api.delete(url, as: T)</code></th><td>Explicit DELETE; <code>body</code> is optional.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><code>as: T</code> declares the required response contract: supported scalars, immutable lists and records. Request bodies must match their static types. Inputs may use props, local state and context, but cannot depend on another request's result.</p>
<table><caption>Request options</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Default and limits</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>timeout_ms</code></th><td>10000; integer literal from 1 to 120000. Covers retries and response reading.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>retries</code> (GET)</th><td>0; integer literal from 0 to 3. Retries network failures, HTTP 429 and 5xx only. Writes do not retry.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>cache_ms</code> (GET)</th><td>0; integer literal from 0 to 60000. Opt-in memory cache belongs to the mounted application.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>dedupe</code> (GET)</th><td><code>true</code>; boolean literal. Matching reads share transport while retaining independent cancellation.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row"><code>invalidate</code> (mutation)</th><td><code>[]</code>; explicit local load names, for example <code>[products]</code>. Success clears the root cache and reloads these reads.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Render resource states</h3>
<p>Both resources expose <code>loading</code>, <code>ready</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>error</code> and <code>data</code>. Reads start on mount; mutations begin idle. Only mutations set <code>success</code>. Starting a request clears previous data and errors.</p>
<p>Read <code>data</code> inside <code>if/elif resource.ready:</code> or a mutation's <code>success</code> branch. Inspect errors inside <code>if/elif resource.error != none:</code>. Compound conditions do not narrow data yet. Error fields are <code>kind: str</code>, <code>message: str</code> and <code>status: int</code>; status is zero except for HTTP errors. Kinds are <code>Http</code>, <code>Network</code>, <code>Timeout</code>, <code>Cancelled</code>, <code>Validation</code>, <code>Limit</code> and <code>Security</code>.</p>
<p>Use <code>read.reload</code> to bypass cache, <code>resource.cancel</code> to cancel a consumer, and <code>write.run</code> to start a mutation. Pass these method references to event handlers, as in the example above, or call them without arguments inside an action. Repeated runs during an active write are ignored. Cancelling a write cannot undo a server commit.</p>
<h3>Connect a backend</h3>
<p>Use same-origin absolute paths such as <code>/api/products</code>. Cross-origin URLs, redirects and custom headers are unsupported. HTTPS is required outside loopback development. Responses are limited to 1 MiB and serialized request bodies to 64 KiB.</p>
<p>Cookies are sent only to the same origin. For writes, the backend must inject <code>&lt;meta name="nexo-csrf-token" content="..."&gt;</code> into the served HTML; the runtime sends its value as <code>X-CSRF-Token</code>. The backend must validate both token and Origin, authenticate the session and authorize each resource. The static demo cannot create this session or token; use the downloadable local server to exercise writes.</p>
